{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CfX8XHixssDRzRRCGSyJD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe structure is coherent, additive, and idiomatic for this codebase.\n\n- **Logic is in the right unit.** Customer-code construction already lived in `ClinicServiceImpl` (the old `nextCustomerCode`/`prefix3`), so moving the postcode→region table and `regionForPostcode` there — right next to `deriveCustomerCode`, which is the sole consumer that builds the code — puts the region logic where the identity is minted. `sha256Hex8` is a small private static helper matching the existing `prefix3` pattern, so it doesn't push the service into new-responsibility territory.\n\n- **No duplication; single source of truth.** The old design had region logic in the mapper *and* the code-building logic in the service. The new design derives the region once (in `deriveCustomerCode`), bakes it into the `customerCode`, and has `OwnerMapper.locality()` simply read back the `REGION` segment. That eliminates the parallel city/postcode tables the mapper used to carry (the `java.util.Map` import removal is correct and complete) and makes `locality()` a trivial, honest parse of the canonical identity — exactly the \"locality must move to the new identity\" the spec asked for.\n\n- **Natural for a maintainer.** `UNKNOWN_REGION` is factored to a constant, `regionForPostcode` keeps its focused Javadoc, and the mapper/service split (build in service, read in mapper) is a clean contract.\n\nTwo non-blocking notes, neither worth gating on:\n- The new `import java.util.Map;` is inserted before `java.util.Collection`, breaking the otherwise-alphabetical `java.util.*` ordering; ideally it sits between `List` and `Objects`.\n- `locality()` now implicitly depends on the `\"<REGION>-<HASH8>\"` format produced by the service — this coupling is spec-mandated and is the intended single-source design, just worth being aware of."}]